#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Duty Board v3.174.0 — HR 1 + 2 PRODUCT-FIRST REDO (voice correction
Phase 6, passes 1 and 2 of 8).

Style v3 in the strict flat manual register: imperative procedure
voice, numbered field walks, no narrative flourishes. Replaces lessons
AND question banks for employee_master and recruitment in place.

Deploy: apply -> commit -> then on the server:
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_hr_pro.refresh_lessons --kwargs "{'only': 'employee_master'}"
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_hr_pro.refresh_questions --kwargs "{'only': 'employee_master'}"
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_hr_pro.refresh_lessons --kwargs "{'only': 'recruitment'}"
  bench --site xlevel.clouderp.one execute duty_board.academy_seed_hr_pro.refresh_questions --kwargs "{'only': 'recruitment'}"

Anchored, idempotent. Requires v3.173.0.
Run from ~/frappe-bench/apps/duty_board.
"""

import io
import json
import os
import sys

INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_hr_pro_data.json"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}
Q = lambda q, opts, ans, why, src: {"q": q, "opts": opts, "ans": ans, "why": why, "src": src}

# ============================== HR 1 ==============================
LESSONS_H1 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Employee master: scope and structure", "11", "<p>This module covers the Employee master in ZhiftERP and the organisational masters it depends on. On completing this module you will be able to: create the organisational masters (Company, Branch, Department, Designation, Employment Type, Grade); create a complete Employee record and explain the purpose of each field group; operate the status lifecycle; build the reporting structure and read the organisational chart; and run the register hygiene checks.</p><p><b>The Employee record's position in the system.</b> Employee is a master doctype. The following records link to it:</p><p>1. <b>Salary Structure Assignment and Salary Slip</b> (the Payroll track) — pay is computed for Employee records.<br>2. <b>Leave Allocation, Leave Application, and Attendance</b> (this track, modules 3-4) — leave and attendance are recorded against Employee records.<br>3. <b>Appraisal, Training Event, and the lifecycle documents</b> (modules 5-7).<br>4. <b>The User account</b> — the Employee record links to the person's login; this link scopes employee self-service (the Payroll track, module 4).<br>5. <b>Expense Claim, Employee Advance, and asset custody records.</b></p><p>Because every one of these reads the Employee record, an error in the record repeats in every linked document until corrected. The verification procedure in Chapter 5 exists for this reason.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> HR owns the Employee register: creation, changes, and status. The permission design follows the Sysadmin track's rules: HR roles hold Create and Write on Employee; other functions hold Read where their work requires it; the employee sees their own record through self-service. Changes to bank details and contact details follow the governed master-data rules stated in the Payroll track (written instruction from the employee, filed on the record).</p><p><b>Build order.</b> The organisational masters are created before Employee records, because Employee fields link to them. The order used in this module:</p><p>1. Company (exists from implementation).<br>2. Branch — one record per physical location.<br>3. Department — the tree, with each department linked to its company.<br>4. Designation — one record per job title.<br>5. Employment Type — the employment categories (Full-time, Part-time, Contract, Intern, and others the business defines).<br>6. Grade — where the business uses grades, the Employee Grade records with their default salary structures.<br>7. Employee records, in Chapter 3's field order.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the organisational masters. Chapter 3: the Employee record — identity and joining fields. Chapter 4: the organisational fields. Chapter 5: contact, personal, and bank fields, with the verification procedure. Chapter 6: the status lifecycle and exit fields. Chapter 7: the reporting structure and the organisational chart. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The organisational masters", "12", "<p>Create each master below before creating Employee records. Each section states the doctype, the fields to complete, and the records that read it.</p><p><b>1. Branch.</b> Open Branch (awesome bar) &gt; New. Enter the branch name. Create one record per physical location. Read by: the Employee record's Branch field; branch filters on HR reports; the Payroll Entry's branch filter.</p><p><b>2. Department.</b> Open Department &gt; New. Fields:</p><p>• <b>Department name.</b><br>• <b>Company</b> — required; departments are per company.<br>• <b>Parent Department</b> — departments form a tree. Set Is Group on parent nodes that contain sub-departments. Example structure: All Departments &gt; Operations &gt; Warehouse; All Departments &gt; Finance.<br>• <b>Leave Approver and Expense Approver tables</b> — the users authorised to approve leave applications and expense claims for this department's employees. Module 4 reads the leave approvers; complete these tables when the approvers are known.<br>• <b>Payroll Cost Center</b> — where the site is configured to default the employee's cost center from the department, set it here. The Payroll track's cost-addressing depends on this value or the per-employee field.</p><p>Read by: the Employee record's Department field; department filters on every HR report; the approval routing in modules 3-4.</p><p><b>3. Designation.</b> Open Designation &gt; New. Enter the designation name and, where useful, the required skills and description. Create one record per job title in use — Pharmacist, Warehouse Assistant, Branch Manager, Driver, Accounts Assistant. Read by: the Employee record; Job Opening and Job Offer (module 2); Appraisal templates (module 5). Do not encode seniority into free text on the employee; the Designation record is the single definition of each title.</p><p><b>4. Employment Type.</b> Open Employment Type &gt; New. Create the employment categories the business uses: Full-time, Part-time, Contract, Intern, Probation where the business tracks it as a type. Read by: the Employee record; headcount reports by type; policy rules that differ by category (leave entitlement for contract staff, for example — module 4's leave policies can differ by type).</p><p><b>5. Employee Grade.</b> Where the business uses grades: open Employee Grade &gt; New. Enter the grade name and the <b>Default Salary Structure</b> where grades map to pay packages — this default pre-fills at assignment and is verified against the offer letter per the Payroll track's rules. Read by: the Employee record's Grade field; grade-based reports and policies.</p><p><b>Verification.</b> After creating the masters, run these checks:</p><p>1. The Department tree renders correctly (open the Department list in tree view; every node under the correct parent; Is Group set on parents only).<br>2. Every department carries its company.<br>3. The Designation list contains one record per real job title, no duplicates, no obsolete titles left active.<br>4. Approver tables completed on departments where the approvers are known; a note recorded for those pending.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Employee record: identity and joining fields", "12", "<p>Open Employee (awesome bar) &gt; New. This chapter walks the identity and joining fields. Complete them in order.</p><p><b>1. Series.</b> The Employee record is named by naming series (HR-EMP- by default). The series is configured once in the Naming Series tool per the Sysadmin track's rules. Do not rename Employee records after creation; the ID appears on every linked document.</p><p><b>2. First Name, Middle Name, Last Name.</b> Enter the legal names as they appear on the employee's identification documents. The full name prints on the salary slip, the statutory schedules, and every HR document; a name entered casually here is a name wrong on a PAYE certificate later.</p><p><b>3. Gender and Date of Birth.</b> Enter from the identification documents. Date of Birth feeds the retirement-date computation where the business configures a retirement age, and the birthday reads on HR reports.</p><p><b>4. Date of Joining.</b> The first day of employment, from the signed offer or contract. This field is read by:</p><p>• The payment-days proration on the first Salary Slip (the Payroll track, module 1).<br>• Leave allocation proration for the joining year (module 4).<br>• Length-of-service computations on reports and at exit.</p><p>Enter the exact contractual date. Do not enter the date the record was created.</p><p><b>5. Status.</b> New records are created as Active. The full lifecycle is Chapter 6.</p><p><b>6. Company.</b> The employing legal entity. On a multi-company site this field determines which company's payroll, leave policies, and reports include the employee. A person employed by two entities is two Employee records (the Payroll track, module 8).</p><p><b>7. The joining detail fields.</b> Complete the employment section: the offer date and confirmation date where tracked; the contract end date for fixed-term contracts — the contract end date is read by the standing report of expiring contracts, and a blank field there means an expiry nobody is warned about; the notice period in days, read at exit; the probation period where tracked.</p><p><b>8. Attachments.</b> Attach at creation: the signed offer or contract, the identification document, and the completed joining forms. The record with its attachments is the employment file's system copy; module 2's onboarding procedure (in the recruitment module) generates part of this automatically when the hire comes through the recruitment pipeline.</p><p><b>Save.</b> Save the record. The organisational fields (Chapter 4) and the contact, personal, and bank sections (Chapter 5) complete the record before the joiner verification (Chapter 5) signs it off.</p><p><b>Note on record creation paths.</b> An Employee record is created either directly (this chapter) or from an accepted Job Applicant through the recruitment pipeline (module 2, Chapter 7). The fields and the verification are identical on both paths; the pipeline pre-fills what the applicant record already holds.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The Employee record: organisational fields", "12", "<p>Complete the organisational section of the Employee record. Each field below links to a Chapter 2 master and drives specific behaviour.</p><p><b>1. Branch.</b> Select the employee's branch. Read by branch filters on attendance, leave, and payroll reports.</p><p><b>2. Department.</b> Select from the department tree. Read by: report grouping; the approver routing (the department's Leave Approver and Expense Approver tables apply to this employee unless a per-employee approver overrides them); the cost-center default where configured at department level.</p><p><b>3. Designation.</b> Select the job title. Read by reports, the appraisal machinery (module 5), and the recruitment records when the position is later re-hired.</p><p><b>4. Reports To.</b> Select the employee's direct manager (another Employee record). This field builds the reporting hierarchy:</p><p>• The <b>Organizational Chart</b> page (Chapter 7) renders from this field.<br>• Approval routing can follow it where the site is configured to route to the reporting manager.<br>• Every employee except the top of the structure has a value here. A blank Reports To on a non-executive record is a data gap; the Chapter 8 verification lists them.</p><p><b>5. Grade.</b> Select where grades are in use. Where the grade carries a Default Salary Structure, the payroll assignment pre-fills from it and is verified against the offer letter.</p><p><b>6. Employment Type.</b> Select the employment category. Read by headcount reports and type-dependent policies.</p><p><b>7. Holiday List.</b> Select the employee's working calendar. This field is read by:</p><p>• Payment-days proration (the Payroll track, module 1) — working days count from this list.<br>• Leave application day counts (module 4) — holidays inside a leave period do not consume leave balance when the list says so.<br>• Attendance processing (module 3) — holidays are not absence.</p><p>Assign the correct list per working pattern: the standard list for head office; the branch list for locations that work Saturdays. The Holiday List records themselves are built in module 3.</p><p><b>8. Payroll Cost Center.</b> Where the site addresses payroll cost per employee, select the branch or unit cost center. The Payroll track, module 1, Chapter 2 states the consequence: a blank value posts salary cost to the company fallback and distorts the branch profit reads. Where the department-level default is configured instead, verify the department carries it.</p><p><b>9. Leave Approver and Expense Approver (per-employee).</b> Where a specific employee's approvals route differently from their department's tables, set the per-employee approver fields. Otherwise leave them blank and let the department tables govern.</p><p><b>Verification.</b> After completing the section, confirm: every organisational field selected from its master (no blanks except documented ones); Reports To present; Holiday List matching the actual working pattern; the cost-center value present at whichever level the site's design placed it.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Contact, personal, and bank sections; the joiner verification", "12", "<p>Complete the remaining sections, then run the verification procedure.</p><p><b>1. Contact details.</b></p><p>• <b>Preferred Contact Email</b> — select Company Email or Personal Email, and complete the selected field. This is the address the system uses for salary slip delivery (the Payroll track, module 4) and HR notifications. Verify the address by sending one test message before relying on it.<br>• <b>Mobile</b> — the employee's number, used for records and for the identity call-back rule (the Payroll track's confidentiality procedures call back the number on record).<br>• <b>Current Address and Permanent Address</b> — enter from the employee's declaration. The <b>state of residence</b> derived from the current address is read by the PAYE state-split (the Payroll track, module 2, Chapter 5); it is mandatory at joining and updated only on the employee's written notice.<br>• <b>Emergency contact</b> — name, relation, number. Verify the number is complete; this field is read once, on the day it matters.</p><p><b>2. Personal details.</b> Marital status, blood group where tracked, and the identification numbers the business records (national identification, tax identification for the statutory chain). Enter identification numbers exactly as printed on the documents and attach the document scans.</p><p><b>3. Bank details.</b> Bank Name and Bank A/C No. Rules, restated from the Payroll track:</p><p>1. Enter from the employee's own written instruction; attach the instruction to the record.<br>2. Changes are governed events: verified with the employee through a known channel, never actioned from a forwarded message.<br>3. The Salary Mode field (Bank, Cash, Cheque) is completed with the bank fields.</p><p><b>4. Health and other sections.</b> Complete per the business's policy on what is recorded. Health details are sensitive personal data; the access rules on the Employee doctype govern who can read them, and the register hygiene read (Chapter 8) confirms the permission matrix covers them.</p><p><b>The joiner verification procedure.</b> Run before the employee's first payroll and before self-service access is issued:</p><p>1. Open the Employee record beside the physical or scanned file: the offer letter, the identification documents, the bank instruction, the address declaration.<br>2. Confirm each field group against its source: names and dates against identification; Date of Joining against the contract; bank fields against the instruction; addresses against the declaration.<br>3. Confirm the organisational fields (Chapter 4's verification list).<br>4. Confirm the attachments are on the record.<br>5. Record the verification in the record's comments: date, verified by. The Payroll track's run checklist (its module 3, line 1) assumes this comment exists.</p><p>The verification takes approximately five minutes per joiner. Every downstream document inherits any error this procedure would have caught.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The status lifecycle and exit fields", "12", "<p>The Status field controls whether the employee participates in the system's processes. This chapter states each status, what sets it, and what reads it.</p><p><b>The statuses.</b></p><p>1. <b>Active</b> — the working state. The employee appears in payroll runs (Get Employees selects Active employees with assignments), leave allocations, attendance processing, and headcount reports.<br>2. <b>Inactive</b> — a non-working state without termination, used per the business's policy for long unpaid absence categories where the site distinguishes them. An Inactive employee is excluded from runs. Document the reason in the record when using this status.<br>3. <b>Suspended</b> — where the business's disciplinary policy uses suspension and the site tracks it as a status. The pay consequence of a suspension is a decision executed through the Payroll track's lanes (leave without pay records, or the documented decision), not an automatic effect of the status alone; confirm the site's configuration and record the decision.<br>4. <b>Left</b> — the exit state. Set with the Relieving Date.</p><p><b>The exit fields.</b> On confirmation of an exit (the Payroll track, module 7 holds the full exit procedure; this section is the HR side):</p><p>1. <b>Relieving Date</b> — the last day of service, from the accepted resignation or the decision letter. Read by: the final slip's proration; the leave balance cutoff and encashment; exclusion from subsequent runs.<br>2. <b>Status → Left</b> — set effective the relieving date.<br>3. <b>Reason for Leaving</b> — select or enter per the business's categories (resignation, contract end, termination, retirement). Read by the attrition reports.<br>4. <b>Leave Encashed</b> — where the exit includes encashment, the flag and its linked Leave Encashment record (the Payroll track, module 7, Chapter 3).<br>5. <b>Exit interview fields</b> — where the business records exit interviews, complete the fields or attach the interview record. Module 7 of this track covers the exit interview procedure.</p><p><b>The two-field rule.</b> Relieving Date and Status are set the day the exit is confirmed. The Payroll track states the consequence of delay: a leaver who remains Active continues to be selected by payroll runs. The HR action and the payroll checklist line reference the same two fields.</p><p><b>Status changes are recorded events.</b> Every status change carries a comment or an attached document stating the reason and the authority: the resignation letter, the decision letter, the policy reference for an Inactive period. The Version history on the Employee record (track changes is confirmed on for Employee per the Sysadmin track's audit rules) records who changed the status and when; the comment records why.</p><p><b>What is never done.</b> Employee records are not deleted. A record with history is retired by status, never removed; deletion breaks every linked document and removes the employment history the statutory retention rules require the business to keep. The Deleted Document review (the Sysadmin track, module 8) treats a deleted Employee record as a finding.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The reporting structure and the organisational chart", "12", "<p>The Reports To field (Chapter 4) builds the reporting structure. This chapter covers reading it, maintaining it, and the approval routing that uses it.</p><p><b>The Organizational Chart page.</b> Open Organizational Chart (awesome bar). The page renders the hierarchy from the Reports To fields: each node an employee, children the employees who report to them. Use the page for:</p><p>1. <b>Structure verification</b> — the rendered chart matches the intended structure; a misplaced node is a wrong Reports To value, corrected on the Employee record.<br>2. <b>Orphan detection</b> — employees missing from the tree below the top node have blank or wrong Reports To values.<br>3. <b>Span reading</b> — the number of direct reports per manager is visible per node; the business reads it for structure decisions.</p><p><b>Maintaining the structure.</b> Reporting changes are Employee-record edits with the standing disciplines:</p><p>1. A manager change is an edit to Reports To on each affected report, made on the documented decision (the transfer memo, the promotion record — module 7 of this track covers the lifecycle documents that carry these changes).<br>2. A manager's exit re-points their reports before or at the relieving date. Add this line to the exit checklist for any leaver with direct reports: the Organizational Chart page after the exit shows no orphaned nodes.<br>3. Department moves and Reports To changes are made together when a transfer changes both; the Employee Transfer document (module 7) applies both properties from its date.</p><p><b>Approval routing.</b> Two routing designs exist on the site; know which is configured:</p><p>1. <b>Department-table routing</b> — leave applications and expense claims route to the approvers listed on the employee's Department (Chapter 2). This design survives manager changes without per-employee edits.<br>2. <b>Per-employee routing</b> — the Leave Approver / Expense Approver fields on the Employee record name the approver directly. This design overrides or replaces the department tables per the site's configuration.</p><p>The maintenance consequence differs: department-table routing is maintained on a handful of Department records; per-employee routing must be swept when an approver leaves or moves. The register hygiene read (Chapter 8) includes the sweep for sites using per-employee routing: filter the Employee list by each approver field value and confirm every named approver is a current, appropriate holder.</p><p><b>The employee-number reads.</b> Two standing lookups from the Employee list serve the structure:</p><p>1. <b>Headcount by dimension</b> — the list grouped by Company, Branch, Department, Designation, Employment Type, or Grade answers headcount questions without a report build. Filter Status = Active for current headcount.<br>2. <b>The joiners and leavers lists</b> — filtered by Date of Joining or Relieving Date within the period. The Payroll track's headcount reconciliation (its module 3) uses these lists; HR produces them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: the HealthTrade employee master", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to HealthTrade (the pharmaceutical distributor used across the programme: Head Office plus three branches — Central/Lagos, Ibadan, Akure — approximately thirty staff). Configuration values and findings are listed per step.</p><p><b>Step 1 — organisational masters (Chapter 2).</b></p><p>• Branch: four records — Head Office, Central Lagos, Ibadan, Akure.<br>• Department tree: All Departments &gt; Operations (Is Group) &gt; Warehouse, Dispatch; All Departments &gt; Retail (Is Group) &gt; Pharmacy Counter; All Departments &gt; Finance; All Departments &gt; Administration. Each department linked to the company. Leave Approver tables completed on Operations, Retail, and Finance with the respective managers.<br>• Designation: eleven records covering the titles in use (Pharmacist, Pharmacy Technician, Warehouse Assistant, Driver, Branch Manager, Accounts Assistant, and others). Two obsolete titles from the implementation era found in the list and disabled.<br>• Employment Type: Full-time, Contract, Intern.<br>• Grade: not used; the field left ungoverned by decision, recorded.</p><p><b>Step 2 — the record sweep (Chapters 3-5).</b> The thirty existing Employee records are verified against files using the Chapter 5 procedure. Findings, each corrected on the record with its source attached:</p><p>1. Four records with Personal Email entered but Preferred Contact Email unset — delivery had been failing silently for these four; set and test-messaged.<br>2. Two records with blank Payroll Cost Center (already found by the Payroll track's walk; corrected there; confirmed here).<br>3. Three records with Date of Joining equal to the record-creation date rather than the contractual date — corrected from the contracts; one produced a leave-allocation correction in module 4.<br>4. Six records without identification attachments — collected and attached.<br>5. One bank account number failing verification against the written instruction — corrected (also found in the Payroll track's walk).</p><p><b>Step 3 — the reporting structure (Chapter 7).</b> Reports To completed on all thirty records. The Organizational Chart page renders: the MD at the top; three branch managers and the finance lead reporting to the MD; branch staff under their managers. Findings: two warehouse staff pointed at a supervisor who left the previous year — re-pointed to the current supervisor; one node orphaned by a blank Reports To — completed.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the routing decision (Chapter 7).</b> Department-table routing is confirmed as the design (fewer records to maintain). The per-employee approver fields are swept and cleared where they duplicated the department tables; one deliberate override retained and documented (a head-office employee whose leave routes to the MD directly).</p><p><b>Step 5 — the standing reads instituted.</b> Monthly: the joiners and leavers lists produced for the payroll reconciliation; the blank-field filters run (Reports To, Holiday List, Preferred Contact Email, Payroll Cost Center — target zero blanks); the contract-expiry report read for the ninety-day window. Quarterly: the approver sweep confirmed against the department tables; the attachment completeness sample (five records checked against files).</p><p><b>Result.</b> Thirty verified records, eleven corrections applied at the sweep, four standing monthly filters at zero blanks, and the organisational chart rendering the actual structure. The register is ready for modules 3-4, which read it every day.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur on the Employee register, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the incomplete record.</b> Symptom: payroll, leave, or delivery failures traced to blank fields (Holiday List, Preferred Contact Email, Payroll Cost Center, Reports To). Correction: complete the field from its source document. Prevention: the joiner verification procedure (Chapter 5) before first payroll, and the monthly blank-field filters (Chapter 8) at target zero.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the record-creation date entered as Date of Joining.</b> Symptom: first-month proration wrong; leave allocation wrong for the joining year; length-of-service reports wrong. Correction: the contractual date entered from the contract; downstream corrections through the Payroll track's lanes where a slip already computed. Prevention: the verification procedure checks the date against the contract explicitly.</p><p><b>Error 3 — organisational data in free text.</b> Symptom: job titles, branches, or types typed as text variants instead of selected from masters; reports that cannot group. Correction: the master record created or corrected; the employee fields re-selected. Prevention: the build order (masters before employees) and the Sysadmin track's rule that reportable fields are links to masters.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the stale reporting structure.</b> Symptom: the organisational chart showing departed managers; approvals routing to people who left; orphaned nodes. Correction: Reports To re-pointed on the affected records. Prevention: the leaver checklist line for any exit with direct reports, and the chart read after each such exit.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the unrecorded status change.</b> Symptom: statuses changed without documented reason or authority; an Inactive period nobody can explain; a Left status with no relieving date. Correction: the document obtained and attached; the fields completed. Prevention: the rule that every status change carries its comment and document, verified by the Version history.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the deleted record.</b> Symptom: an Employee record removed, breaking linked documents and the retention obligation. Correction: restoration through the platform's recovery paths and the Sysadmin track's incident procedure. Prevention: Delete withheld from HR daily roles in the permission matrix; retirement by status only; the Deleted Document review treating any occurrence as a finding.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state what links to the Employee record and the consequence of register errors; create the five organisational masters in build order with their key fields; complete the Employee record's identity, joining, organisational, contact, personal, and bank sections, stating what reads each field; run the joiner verification procedure; operate the status lifecycle with the two-field exit rule and the recorded-change rule; maintain the reporting structure and read the Organizational Chart page; and run the monthly and quarterly hygiene reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 1: the Employee record is the master every HR and payroll document reads — built from the organisational masters, completed from source documents, verified before first use, retired by status, and kept complete by the standing filters.</b> Module 2 covers recruitment: the pipeline from Staffing Plan to Employee Onboarding.</p>"),
]

