# ============================== HR 2 ==============================
LESSONS_H2 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The recruitment pipeline", "11", "<p>This module covers recruitment in ZhiftERP: the document pipeline from planned vacancy to onboarded employee. On completing this module you will be able to: create a Staffing Plan; create and manage Job Openings; process Job Applicants through their status pipeline; schedule and record Interviews with structured feedback; issue Job Offers; create the Employee record from an accepted applicant; and run Employee Onboarding from a template.</p><p><b>The pipeline.</b> Recruitment runs through six doctypes, in order. Learn the sequence; each chapter walks one stage.</p><p>1. <b>Staffing Plan</b> — the approved vacancies per designation for a period, with estimated costs (Chapter 2).<br>2. <b>Job Opening</b> — one advertised position (Chapter 3).<br>3. <b>Job Applicant</b> — one candidate for an opening, with a status pipeline (Chapter 4).<br>4. <b>Interview</b> — scheduled assessment events with structured feedback (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>Job Offer</b> — the offer document with its terms table and status (Chapter 6).<br>6. <b>Employee + Employee Onboarding</b> — the record created from the accepted applicant, and the onboarding task list generated from a template (Chapter 7).</p><p><b>Why the pipeline is used rather than informal hiring.</b> Each stage produces a record, and the records produce three things informal hiring cannot:</p><p>1. <b>Traceability</b> — every hire traces from its vacancy through its interviews to its offer; every offer's terms are on a document; the Employee record created at the end pre-fills from verified pipeline data instead of retyped notes.<br>2. <b>Control</b> — hiring against the Staffing Plan means vacancies are approved before they are advertised; offers are issued from a document with an approval, not from a conversation.<br>3. <b>Reporting</b> — time-to-hire, source effectiveness, and pipeline counts read from the records.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> HR operates the pipeline. The hiring decisions belong to the deciding managers and the owner per the business's approval design: the Staffing Plan's approval authorises the headcount and budget; the offer's terms follow the approved decision. The operator's rule from the Payroll track applies unchanged: no record without its approved decision, and the record states exactly what the decision stated.</p><p><b>Interaction with module 1.</b> The pipeline ends where module 1 begins: the Employee record. The joiner verification (module 1, Chapter 5) runs identically for pipeline hires; the pipeline reduces the retyping, not the verification.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: Staffing Plan. Chapter 3: Job Opening. Chapter 4: Job Applicant. Chapter 5: Interview. Chapter 6: Job Offer. Chapter 7: Employee creation and Employee Onboarding. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Staffing Plan", "12", "<p>Open Staffing Plan (awesome bar) &gt; New. The Staffing Plan records the approved vacancies for a period. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Name and Company.</b> Name the plan for its period and scope: <i>2026 H2 — Operations</i>. Select the company.</p><p><b>2. From Date and To Date.</b> The period the plan covers. Job Openings created against the plan are validated within this period.</p><p><b>3. The Staffing Details table.</b> One row per designation being planned:</p><p>• <b>Designation</b> — from the Designation master (module 1, Chapter 2).<br>• <b>Number of Positions</b> — the approved vacancy count for the period.<br>• <b>Current Count</b> — the current headcount in that designation; the system computes the current count from Active Employee records where configured, and the row shows current against planned.<br>• <b>Estimated Cost Per Position</b> — the annual cost estimate used for the plan's budget total.<br>• <b>Total Estimated Cost</b> — computed per row; the plan totals the rows.</p><p><b>4. Department scoping.</b> Where the plan is scoped to a department, set it; company-wide plans leave the scope broad. Use one plan per approval decision — a plan is an approved document, and its rows are the approved numbers.</p><p><b>5. Submit.</b> The Staffing Plan is a submittable document. Submission records the approval state; amendments follow document rules.</p><p><b>What the plan controls.</b> With staffing plans in use, Job Opening creation validates against the plan: an opening for a designation is checked against the planned positions for the period, and openings beyond the plan are blocked or flagged per the site's configuration. This is the control stated in Chapter 1: vacancies are approved before they are advertised. Where the validation blocks a genuinely needed opening, the correction is a plan amendment through its approval, not a bypass.</p><p><b>The plan's reads.</b></p><p>1. <b>Plan versus actual</b> — the plan's rows against current Active headcount per designation: positions filled, positions open, positions over plan.<br>2. <b>The budget read</b> — the plan's total estimated cost is the hiring budget's document; the owner approved this number, and the review round's salary modelling (the Payroll track, module 6) reads beside it.<br>3. <b>The period close</b> — at the plan period's end, the plan is read against outcomes: positions filled within period, positions lapsed, cost against estimate. The next period's plan is drafted from this read.</p><p>Practical procedure: draft the plan rows with the deciding managers, attach the approval, submit, and only then open Chapter 3's openings. A hire that starts at the opening instead of the plan has skipped the approval that the pipeline exists to enforce.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Job Opening", "12", "<p>Open Job Opening (awesome bar) &gt; New. One Job Opening is one advertised position. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Job Title.</b> The advertised title. Keep it consistent with the Designation; the advertisement can elaborate, the designation stays canonical.</p><p><b>2. Designation.</b> Select from the Designation master. This link connects the opening to the Staffing Plan validation (Chapter 2) and to the applicant and offer records downstream.</p><p><b>3. Company, Department, and Location.</b> Select the employing company; the department and the branch or location fields per the position. These fields pre-fill downstream records and scope the reports.</p><p><b>4. Staffing Plan.</b> Link the opening to its plan row where plans are in use. The validation runs on this link.</p><p><b>5. Status.</b> Open or Closed. An Open opening accepts applicants; close the opening when the position is filled or withdrawn. Closing is a deliberate act: the pipeline reads count open positions from this status, and a filled position left Open overstates the vacancy report.</p><p><b>6. Description.</b> The role description: responsibilities, requirements, and terms summary as the business publishes them. Write the description once here; the applicant-facing publication and the interview rounds (Chapter 5) both read from it.</p><p><b>7. Publication.</b> Where the site publishes openings to the website portal, the publish setting on the opening controls listing. Where the business advertises externally (job boards, referrals), record the channels used — Chapter 4's source tracking reads them.</p><p><b>The opening's lifecycle.</b></p><p>1. Created against the plan; status Open.<br>2. Applicants attach to it (Chapter 4) as they apply.<br>3. Interviews and offers proceed for shortlisted applicants (Chapters 5-6).<br>4. On acceptance and joining, the opening is Closed with a note of the hire; on withdrawal, Closed with the reason.</p><p><b>The openings read.</b> The Job Opening list filtered by Status = Open is the live vacancy report: each row's age (creation date to today) is the time-open figure, and openings aging beyond the business's expected time-to-fill are flagged to the hiring manager. Run this read monthly with the plan-versus-actual read (Chapter 2).</p><p><b>One opening per position.</b> Create one opening per vacancy instance where positions are tracked individually, or one opening with the position count per the business's practice — decide once and record the convention, because the vacancy reports count differently under each convention and a mixed register counts nothing reliably.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The Job Applicant", "12", "<p>Open Job Applicant (awesome bar) &gt; New — or receive records created by the portal where applications arrive online. One record per candidate per opening. Complete or verify the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Applicant Name and Email Address.</b> The candidate's name and contact email. The email is the pipeline's communication channel; verify it is entered correctly on manually created records.</p><p><b>2. Job Opening.</b> Link the applicant to the opening applied for. A candidate applying for two openings is two applicant records; the records track independently.</p><p><b>3. Source.</b> Select the Job Applicant Source — the channel that produced the candidate (Website, Referral, the named job board, Walk-in). Create the source records once in the Job Applicant Source master. The source field feeds the effectiveness read: applicants and hires per source, which tells the business where its advertising money produces hires.</p><p><b>4. The resume and documents.</b> Attach the candidate's resume and any submitted documents to the record. The record is the candidate's file; interviewers read from it (Chapter 5), and the offer stage reads it again.</p><p><b>5. Status.</b> The applicant status pipeline. The standard values and their use:</p><p>1. <b>Open</b> — received, not yet processed.<br>2. <b>Replied</b> — contact made; in process (screening, interviewing).<br>3. <b>Hold</b> — parked with a reason recorded (a strong candidate for a later vacancy; a process pause).<br>4. <b>Rejected</b> — not proceeding; set with the courtesy communication sent. Reject formally rather than by silence: unanswered applicants judge the business by the silence, and the status left Open forever makes the pipeline reads meaningless.<br>5. <b>Accepted</b> — offer accepted; proceeds to Employee creation (Chapter 7).</p><p><b>The pipeline reads.</b></p><p>1. <b>The funnel count</b> — applicants per status per opening: how many received, in process, on hold, rejected, accepted. Read weekly during active hiring; a stage where candidates accumulate without moving is the process bottleneck to fix.<br>2. <b>The aging read</b> — days since last status change per active applicant. Candidates aging at Open are unprocessed applications; candidates aging at Replied are a slow interview stage.<br>3. <b>The source read</b> — applicants and eventual hires grouped by Source, quarterly.</p><p><b>Data handling.</b> Applicant records contain personal data of people who may never join the business. Apply the standing confidentiality rules: access limited to the recruiting roles; documents stored on the record, not in personal drives; and a retention convention decided and recorded — how long rejected applicants' records and documents are kept, and the periodic clearing per that convention.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The Interview", "13", "<p>Interviews are scheduled and recorded through two doctypes: the Interview Round (the reusable definition of an assessment stage) and the Interview (one scheduled event for one applicant). Build the rounds once; schedule interviews per applicant.</p><p><b>1. The Interview Round.</b> Open Interview Round (awesome bar) &gt; New. One record per assessment stage per role family:</p><p>• <b>Round Name</b> — <i>Technical Screen — Pharmacist</i>, <i>Final Interview — Management</i>.<br>• <b>Designation</b> — the role family the round applies to.<br>• <b>Interviewers</b> — the default panel: the users who conduct this round.<br>• <b>Expected Skill Set</b> — the skills table: one row per skill the round assesses. This table defines the feedback form's structure (see the Interview Feedback below); write the skills as assessable items (<i>dispensing accuracy</i>, <i>regulatory knowledge</i>, <i>team supervision</i>), because interviewers will rate exactly these rows.</p><p><b>2. The Interview.</b> Open Interview &gt; New per scheduled event:</p><p>• <b>Job Applicant</b> and <b>Interview Round</b> — the candidate and the stage.<br>• <b>Scheduled On, From Time, To Time</b> — the schedule. The interviewers receive the event per the site's notification configuration.<br>• <b>Interviewers</b> — pre-filled from the round; adjust per event where the panel differs.<br>• <b>Status</b> — Pending, Under Review, Cleared, Rejected. Update the status as the event concludes; the applicant's pipeline position (Chapter 4) is read alongside it.</p><p><b>3. The Interview Feedback.</b> Each interviewer submits an Interview Feedback record against the Interview: the skills table from the round rendered as rating rows, plus the overall result and remarks. The design's purpose:</p><p>1. <b>Structured comparison</b> — every candidate for the round is rated on the same skill rows; the comparison across candidates is row by row, not impression against impression.<br>2. <b>Recorded basis</b> — the hire and reject decisions have recorded assessments behind them. Write remarks that state observations, not speculation: what the candidate demonstrated, against the skill row.<br>3. <b>Independent submission</b> — each interviewer submits their own feedback record; panel discussions follow the submissions where the business's practice wants calibration.</p><p><b>The interview procedure, per applicant.</b></p><p>1. Shortlist decision recorded on the applicant (status Replied, remark stating the shortlist).<br>2. Interview created against the applicable round; scheduled; the candidate informed.<br>3. Event conducted; each interviewer submits Interview Feedback the same day (feedback written later is reconstructed impression, not assessment).<br>4. Interview status updated (Cleared or Rejected); the applicant's status and next stage follow.<br>5. Multi-round processes repeat steps 2-4 per round; the applicant's record accumulates the full assessment history.</p><p>Practical procedure: build the Interview Rounds for the standing role families once, with the hiring managers defining the skill rows. A hiring process with defined rounds runs the same regardless of who schedules it, and its records support every later question about how the decision was made.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The Job Offer", "12", "<p>Open Job Offer (awesome bar) &gt; New. The Job Offer is the offer's document of record. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Job Applicant.</b> Select the candidate. The applicant's details pre-fill.</p><p><b>2. Company and Designation.</b> The employing entity and the offered position, from the masters.</p><p><b>3. Offer Date.</b> The date the offer is issued.</p><p><b>4. The Job Offer Terms table.</b> One row per term of the offer: the offer term (from the Offer Term master — Base Salary, Probation Period, Notice Period, Annual Leave, and the other terms the business states) and its value. Rules:</p><p>1. The terms entered here are the terms the approved hiring decision stated — the Chapter 1 custody rule: the record states exactly what the decision stated.<br>2. The pay term entered here is the figure the Payroll track's assignment will carry as Base; the offer document and the assignment must agree, and the joiner verification checks them against each other.<br>3. Terms not in the table are not offered. The table is the complete statement; side communications that vary it create the disputes the document exists to prevent.</p><p><b>5. Status.</b> Awaiting Response → Accepted or Rejected. Update on the candidate's response; the status drives the pipeline reads and Chapter 7's Employee creation.</p><p><b>6. The offer letter print.</b> The Job Offer prints as the offer letter through its print format: the letterhead, the terms table, the validity and conditions text per the business's template. The print rules from the Sysadmin track apply: identity from the letterhead, terms from the document, nothing hard-typed into the format. The signed acceptance (physical or per the business's acceptance process) attaches to the Job Offer record.</p><p><b>The offer procedure.</b></p><p>1. The hiring decision approved per the business's design (the deciding manager and the owner per the approval ladder; the pay figure inside the Staffing Plan's budget or separately approved).<br>2. The Job Offer created with the complete terms table; reviewed against the decision; submitted.<br>3. The offer letter printed and issued; the validity period stated.<br>4. The response recorded: Accepted with the signed acceptance attached, or Rejected with the reason noted (the reasons read quarterly — offers rejected on pay tell the business its figures are off market).<br>5. On acceptance: the applicant status moves to Accepted, and Chapter 7's creation procedure runs.</p><p><b>The offer register read.</b> The Job Offer list is the standing register: offers outstanding (Awaiting Response, with age), acceptance rate per period, and the rejection reasons. Read monthly during active hiring.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Employee creation and Employee Onboarding", "13", "<p>The pipeline's last stage: the accepted applicant becomes an Employee record, and the Employee Onboarding document generates the joining task list.</p><p><b>1. Creating the Employee from the applicant.</b> From the accepted Job Applicant (or the accepted Job Offer per the site's flow), use the create-Employee action. The system creates the Employee record pre-filled with the pipeline's data: name, contact email, designation, company. Then:</p><p>1. Complete the record per module 1, Chapters 3-5: the joining fields (Date of Joining from the accepted offer's agreed start date), the organisational fields, the contact, personal, and bank sections.<br>2. Attach the pipeline documents that belong on the employment file: the signed offer and acceptance (linked via the Job Offer), the identification documents collected at acceptance.<br>3. Run the joiner verification (module 1, Chapter 5) — identical for pipeline hires; the pipeline pre-fill reduces retyping, not verification.<br>4. Close the Job Opening (Chapter 3) with the hire noted.</p><p><b>2. The Employee Onboarding Template.</b> Open Employee Onboarding Template (awesome bar) &gt; New — built once per role family or company-wide:</p><p>• Company, and the Department/Designation scope where templates differ by role.<br>• <b>The Activities table</b> — one row per onboarding task: the activity name, the <b>user or role responsible</b>, the <b>required days</b> (the deadline relative to the boarding start), and whether the task is <b>required before the joining date</b>. Standard activity rows: employment file completed and verified (HR); workstation and tools prepared (Administration); system access created per the joiner procedure (the sysadmin — the Sysadmin track's joiner checklist is this row's content); payroll assignment created and previewed (the payroll operator — the Payroll track's joiner lines); orientation session scheduled (HR/manager); statutory registrations confirmed (PFA, tax — the accountant/HR).</p><p><b>3. The Employee Onboarding document.</b> Open Employee Onboarding &gt; New (or create from the accepted applicant per the flow):</p><p>• The employee (or applicant), the joining date, the template.<br>• On submission the system generates the activity tasks from the template — a project/task set assigned to the responsible users with their deadlines.<br>• <b>Boarding Status</b> tracks completion: Pending → In Process → Completed. The document is the joining checklist with owners and dates, visible as a list instead of held in one person's memory.</p><p><b>4. The completion read.</b> The onboarding is Completed when every activity closes. Two rules:</p><p>1. Activities marked required-before-joining are confirmed complete before the joining date — the joiner who arrives to no workstation, no access, and no payroll assignment is the failure this document prevents, and the pre-joining check is a dated line, not a hope.<br>2. The onboarding document's completion is read at the first payroll close for the joiner: the Payroll track's checklist line 1 (joiner verified and assigned) corresponds to this document's payroll activities.</p><p>Practical procedure: build the template with each responsible function contributing its own rows — the sysadmin writes the access row's content, the payroll operator the assignment row's — so the template is the cross-functional joining procedure in one document.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: a HealthTrade hire end to end", "13", "<p>This chapter runs one hire through Chapters 2-7. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per stage.</p><p><b>Stage 1 — the plan (Chapter 2).</b> The 2026 H2 Staffing Plan for Retail is drafted with the branch managers: rows for Pharmacist (positions 2, current count 4, estimated cost per position entered from the accountant's package figures) and Pharmacy Technician (positions 1). The MD approves; the plan submits. Total estimated cost recorded as the half-year hiring budget line.</p><p><b>Stage 2 — the opening (Chapter 3).</b> A Job Opening is created: Job Title Pharmacist (Akure), Designation Pharmacist, Department Retail &gt; Pharmacy Counter, location Akure, linked to the plan row, status Open. The description is written once on the opening. The advertisement runs on one job board and the staff referral channel; both exist as Job Applicant Source records.</p><p><b>Stage 3 — the applicants (Chapter 4).</b> Nine applicant records accumulate over three weeks: six from the job board, three referrals. Weekly funnel read: week two shows five records aging at Open — the screening backlog is cleared the same week and the read returns to normal. Three candidates shortlist (status Replied, shortlist remarks); six are formally rejected with the courtesy message, statuses set.</p><p><b>Stage 4 — the interviews (Chapter 5).</b> The Pharmacist role family's two Interview Rounds exist from the build: Technical Screen (skills: dispensing accuracy, regulatory knowledge, inventory handling; panel: the senior pharmacist and the Akure branch manager) and Final Interview (skills: customer handling, team fit, availability; panel: the MD and the branch manager). Three Technical Screens run in one week; each interviewer submits Interview Feedback the same day. Two candidates clear to Final; one is rejected on the recorded ratings. Finals run; one candidate clears with consistent ratings across both interviewers.</p><p><b>Stage 5 — the offer (Chapter 6).</b> The hiring decision approves per the ladder; the Job Offer is created with the full terms table: Base Salary (the figure the assignment will carry), Probation Period six months, Notice Period one month, Annual Leave per policy. The offer letter prints on the letterhead and issues with a two-week validity. The candidate accepts; the signed acceptance attaches; status Accepted. The second finalist is placed on Hold with a remark (a strong candidate for the plan's second position).</p><p><b>Stage 6 — creation and onboarding (Chapter 7).</b> The Employee record is created from the applicant, pre-filled; the joining sections complete from the collected documents; the joiner verification runs and its comment is recorded. The Employee Onboarding document generates from the company template: eight activities across HR, Administration, the sysadmin, and the payroll operator. The pre-joining check on the joining date's eve: workstation ready, access created, payroll assignment created and previewed against the offer (the Base matching the offer's term row). The pharmacist joins on the agreed date; the opening closes with the hire noted; boarding status reaches Completed in the first week.</p><p><b>Stage 7 — the reads.</b> The quarter's recruitment reads: time-to-hire for the position, 41 days from opening to joining; source read, the hire from the job board, two of three shortlists from referrals; the plan-versus-actual row updated (one of two Pharmacist positions filled). The records answer each read without reconstruction.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in the recruitment pipeline, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — hiring outside the plan.</b> Symptom: openings and offers for positions no plan approved; headcount and cost growing without an approval trail. Correction: the plan amended through its approval to cover the position, or the opening withdrawn. Prevention: the plan-first order enforced; the opening's plan link and the plan validation in use.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the informal offer.</b> Symptom: terms communicated in conversation or free-form messages; the Job Offer absent or created after acceptance; the assignment's Base disagreeing with what the candidate was told. Correction: the Job Offer created with the complete terms table and reconciled with the candidate before joining. Prevention: the rule that the offer is issued from the document, and the joiner verification's check that the assignment Base equals the offer's pay term.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the silent rejection.</b> Symptom: applicant records aging at Open indefinitely; candidates never answered; pipeline reads meaningless. Correction: statuses set with the courtesy communication sent. Prevention: the weekly funnel and aging reads during active hiring.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the unstructured interview.</b> Symptom: interviews without rounds or feedback records; decisions with no recorded basis; candidates compared by recollection. Correction: feedback recorded for completed interviews where still possible; rounds built for the role family. Prevention: Interview Rounds with skill rows built before hiring starts, and same-day feedback submission as the standing rule.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the unonboarded joiner.</b> Symptom: the new employee arriving to no access, no workstation, no payroll assignment; the first week spent assembling what should have preceded it. Correction: the missing activities completed immediately through their owners. Prevention: the Employee Onboarding document generated at acceptance, and the pre-joining activities confirmed complete on a dated check before the joining date.</p><p><b>Error 6 — applicant data mishandled.</b> Symptom: resumes and personal documents in personal drives and inboxes; no retention convention; rejected candidates' data kept indefinitely. Correction: documents moved onto the records; the retention convention decided and the clearing performed. Prevention: the data-handling rules of Chapter 4 — access limited, documents on records, retention recorded and executed.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the six-stage pipeline and what each stage's record produces; create a Staffing Plan with its details table and state what the plan validates; create and manage Job Openings with the status discipline and the openings read; process Job Applicants through the status pipeline with the source tracking and the three reads; build Interview Rounds with skill rows and run the interview procedure with same-day structured feedback; issue a Job Offer with the complete terms table and the letter print; and create the Employee from the applicant and run Employee Onboarding from a template with the pre-joining check.</p><p><b>Module rule 2: hiring runs through the pipeline — vacancies approved on the plan, candidates processed on recorded statuses, assessments structured and same-day, offers issued from the document with complete terms, and joiners onboarded on a generated checklist confirmed before the joining date.</b> Module 3 covers attendance and shifts.</p>"),
]

